# Who discovered what? Match four scientists
subtitle: "Match Harvey, Faraday, Noether and Meitner to what they discovered, and hear how Lise Meitner missed a Nobel but got an element."

## Who discovered what? Match four scientists
affirm: playful
text: {"font_size":34}
sources:
    - id: harvey
      title: Exercitatio Anatomica de Motu Cordis et Sanguinis in Animalibus
      authors: William Harvey
      year: 1628
      finding: the heart pumps the blood in a continuous circuit round the body
    - id: faraday
      title: Michael Faraday
      authors: Encyclopaedia Britannica
      year: 2024
      finding: 'in 1821 Faraday made a current-carrying wire rotate round a magnet: the first electric motor'
    - id: noether
      title: Noether's theorem
      authors: Encyclopaedia Britannica
      year: 2024
      finding: Emmy Noether proved in 1918 that every continuous symmetry of a physical system corresponds to a conserved quantity
    - id: meitner
      title: Lise Meitner
      authors: Encyclopaedia Britannica / Nobel Prize Outreach
      year: 2024
      finding: with Otto Frisch she explained nuclear fission (1938-39); the 1944 Nobel Prize in Chemistry went to Otto Hahn alone; element 109, meitnerium, is named after her

    card title "Who discovered what?"

Four scientists, four discoveries. Some of these names you know; some you should. Who found what?
    cards sci cites ["harvey","faraday","noether","meitner"]
        * "William Harvey" match "Blood circulation" match_icon.of "anatomical heart" match_icon.set twemoji
        * "Michael Faraday" match "Electric motor" match_icon.of "high voltage" match_icon.set twemoji
        * "Emmy Noether" match "Symmetry laws" match_icon.of "balance scale" match_icon.set twemoji
        * "Lise Meitner" match "Nuclear fission" match_icon.of "atom symbol" match_icon.set twemoji

    ask "Match each scientist to their discovery: drag from one card to its partner." on sci store m feedback.style warm feedback.perfect "A walking history of science." feedback.good "Nearly a full set." feedback.poor "These four are less famous than they should be." feedback.none "Now you know four names worth knowing."
        right: All {m} right.
        wrong: {m} right. Here are the true pairs.

William Harvey, an English doctor, showed in 1628 that the heart pumps the same blood round and round the body.
    highlight sci_1 sci_m_1

Michael Faraday, a blacksmith's son, built the first electric motor in 1821: a wire that spun round a magnet.
    highlight sci_2 sci_m_2

Emmy Noether proved in 1918 that every symmetry in the laws of nature comes with something conserved, such as energy.
    highlight sci_3 sci_m_3

Einstein called her a creative mathematical genius.
    highlight sci_3

And Lise Meitner, with her nephew Otto Frisch, explained nuclear fission at the end of 1938: an atom's core splitting in two.
    highlight sci_4 sci_m_4

But the Nobel Prize for it went to her colleague Otto Hahn alone.
    text nob "no Nobel" #b5482e cites ["meitner"] x 500 y 270 font_size 34

Science made amends later: element 109 is named meitnerium, after her.
    text mt "element 109" #2f6b8f cites ["meitner"] x 500 y 200 font_size 34
